{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3e7","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c836-a2d8-7092-9401-37d2858a2ec3"},"sequence":78,"recorded_at":1780531400000151,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"a68a1bdc-1b82-44d2-b715-51014e29fd57","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"a68a1bdc-1b82-44d2-b715-51014e29fd57","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"a68a1bdc-1b82-44d2-b715-51014e29fd57"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0c836-d4fd-70e3-823c-31842e80c678"},"task_id":"01a0c836-d4fd-70e3-823c-31842e80c678","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0c836-d4fd-70e3-823c-31842e80c678","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev5.md`.\n1|THIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev4.md and every earlier precondition. Do exactly what is written here.\n2|Rework run for TASK-260830-1c28dz. Revision 4 was reviewed by RUN-260922-950a2e (codex gpt-6-astra low) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED: **two P1**, two P2, one P3. Read TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev4.md first.\n3|Revision 4 closed P1-C (expired and cancelled refreshes are rejected and the bound is enforced) and the narrow lease-divergence half of P1-B, and both stay closed. The runtime entry grid is real progress. **But P1-A and P1-B are the same two findings for the second round running, and that is my fault as much as yours: my briefs named the exact vector the reviewer used, and you fixed exactly that vector.** The narrowest fix always passes the vector it was given. So this brief names the INVARIANT and asks you to enumerate what can reach it, and it deliberately does not hand you a list of cases to patch.\n4|\n5|1. **P1-A — the invariant is: once input is closed, nothing reopens it without a fresh authoritative closure proof.** Not \"the no-record case\", which is what revision 4 fixed. The reviewer then drove two ordinary paths straight through the barrier on unchanged production code: an **existing active document** (the normal case, not a corrupt store) — attach records active, quiesce runs with an instance-write `AfterStage` failure after `lock-session` and `detach-client` have executed and the closure report has persisted, and a later writable attach succeeds with no `-r`; and a **failed stop with stale caller state** — a successful quiesce, then a request-stop with expired authorization carrying `Source=active`, where the stop correctly fails but its failure result overwrites the barrier and a writable attach then succeeds, with no storage fault involved at all. The two writers are `ops.go:169-175`, which trusts any existing state other than `quiescing` and never consults the durable closure proof in that case, and `lifecycle.go:433-437`, which overwrites state from failure results including a request's stale source state.\n6|   **Deliver the census, not three more patches.** Enumerate every writer of the input-barrier state and every reader that decides admission from it, in a table: writer/reader, file:line, what it writes or trusts, and whether it consults the authoritative closure proof. For each row, either a committed multi-call `Execute` sequence that proves the barrier holds, or a stated bound with a reason. Then make the authoritative proof the only thing admission consults — advisory memory, caller-carried source state and failure reporting must not be able to reopen a closed barrier, by construction rather than by case analysis. Row 79's lost-write fail-closed claim must become true as written.\n7|2. **P1-B — the invariant is: one composed authorization, covering every identity the effect names.** Revision 4 joined lease ID and epoch; the reviewer then supplied a valid launch decision for one **bootstrap** while the restore named another, and a valid decision descriptor for one **instance** while the backend restore targeted another. Both returned launch with a nil error and executed `new-session` for the divergent target. `wrapper.go:169-178` joins the lease; `req.Decide`'s identity and descriptor and `req.Restore`'s mutation context remain unjoined before `lc.Execute` at line 145. Matching a lease does not establish that the effect is the restore the decision authorized.\n8|   **Again, the census rather than the next two fields.** List every identity member that the decision and the effect each carry — session, bootstrap, instance, backend generation, materialization, and anything else in those two structures — and for each state whether it is joined, deliberately unjoined with a reason, or refused on disagreement. Then bind the complete set through the landed owners and refuse divergence **before** effects. §4.2 step 3 and §4.C's identity-bearing mutations require one composed authorization, not several independently valid descriptions that happen to agree on one field.\n9|3. **P2-A — fourth ask, and you are two packages of thirty-four in.** The runtime entry grid is the right artefact and it covers `tmuxserver` and `termbind`; the remaining closure is still measured as `(package,test) -> pass/fail/skip`, which is a test inventory. Independent `go list -json ./...` over the nine assigned composing packages yields **34 production packages**, 36 with the test-only importers. Extend the grid you already built to that closure: a fixed corpus, the same seeds and clocks, run against the CR base and the candidate, records of `(package, entry, input class) -> outcome class + refusal code`, diffed on the first three with the outcome as value, every moved or one-sided key named. Candidate-only ADDED rows cannot demonstrate that pre-existing admission or refusal behaviour is unchanged, and 27 retained entry keys plus 23,054 passing test keys do not either — that claim exceeds its measurement and must be withdrawn or proven. Keep the test-verdict inventories, labelled as supplementary.\n10|4. **P2-B — unmeasured refusal clauses still survive the committed suite.** Read the reviewer's table, ship one harness row per surviving plant, and name the committed test that kills each. **P3 — residual inaccurate prose**: correct each item the verdict lists, and recount rather than restating.\n11|\n12|A NOTE ON SCOPE, SO YOU CAN RAISE IT INSTEAD OF ABSORBING IT. This leaf is eight operations plus the exec, probe and spawn adapters, and it is on its fifth revision. If closing items 1 and 2 by construction turns out to need a change in a landed owner outside `internal/tmuxserver` — the state store's contract, the wrapper composition entry, or the binding owner — that is a scope question and not something to solve locally with another guard. **Say so in the results, name the owner and the change, and hand off the rest.** A reviewable slice with an honest boundary is a better revision 5 than a fifth round of local patches.\n13|\n14|PROCESS. Keep the candidate UNCOMMITTED; exactly one leaf's worth of delta on top of the checkpoint; base `d4bd91d0`, and if trunk moves refresh through the managed path and repeat the path-by-path audit. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept, and do NOT touch `internal/traceability` — the final leaf g0pcnt carries the registry. Keep the zero-real-tmux determinism property. Run your own package tests, the full importer closure, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...`, and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands`. Update the results with a finding-by-finding table, the two censuses from items 1 and 2, and the extended outcome grid; update the conformance matrix and the evidence archive, attach the results standalone, verify every attachment by reading it back off the board, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-1c28dz --role developer` so revision 5 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be codex gpt-6-astra low.\n15|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n16|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n17|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n18|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n19|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n20|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n21|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n22|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.","final_result":true}}}
